{"index": 8, "prompt_id": "coding-deduplicate", "response": "```python\ndef unique_in_order(items):\n    seen = set()\n    unique_items = set()\n    for item in items:\n        if item not in seen:\n            seen = set()\n            unique_items = [x for x in items if x not in seen]\n    return unique_items\n```\n\n**Time complexity:** O(n), where n is the number of items in the list. This is because we visit each item at most once.\n\n**How it works:**\n\n1. We create an empty set `unique_items` to store unique items.\n2. We create an empty set `seen` to keep track of items we've already seen.\n3. We go through each item in the `items` list.\n4. If an item is not in `unique_items` (i`), we add it to `unique_items` and mark it as seen.\n5. After going through all items, we return `unique_items` as the result.\n\nThis approach ensures that duplicates are removed while preserving the first-seen order.", "result_sha256": "1e5076fae74155bed37999cf1906dad9980bb780157428c3b944a1e14b07bd2f", "stage": "prompt-result-complete"}
